{:edb/db-before-t 220 :edb/db-after-t 221 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533533 50 #inst "2026-10-11T05:46:30.845Z" 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407242 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407241 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407240 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157494 1050 1020.0 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153910 13194139533533 17592186045585 false] [17592186157616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407239 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157616 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=y(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157616 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = v, y = u(1 + v^{2})$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n$x = t, y = y (t), 0 \\le t \\le 1$.\n\nFind the function $y(t)$." 13194139533533 17592186045585 false] [17592186157616 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) \\\\  & =(v,1+{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=1+{v}^{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=1+{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$" 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153922 13194139533533 17592186045585 false] [17592186157618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407237 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157618 1071 "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y={u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186157618 1071 "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = uv, y = u^{2}$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ$.*" 13194139533533 17592186045585 false] [17592186157618 1074 "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u\\cdot 0,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1\\cdot v,\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u\\cdot 1,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0\\cdot v,\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n \n\n![](images/68/6812a33686ab2647cd0f5d586152c68770ebfcb1b8d85cedd98f6839a8364afd.png)" 13194139533533 17592186045585 true] [17592186158611 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to find the images of simple objects in the plane under the action of nonlinear transformations.\n\nConsider the square region ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) and its boundary ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) in the ![](images/13/13377a2a85887e0a15df06f28ec7e6be35b98579a0865d0292a7f64da0d57b2e.svg)-plane, where ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/f2/f285f8016e64c5ab750d94dac618fe2c1a09ced6f5fce7bbaea85e7258eda2c5.svg)\n\nA sketch of this region, specified with a counterclockwise orientation, is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n     \n\nSince ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) is specified with an orientation, we refer to it as a **directed boundary.**\n\nNow, consider the transformation ![](images/0c/0cedb44f040d579cf90349a6891c23cb11a0cc35833979b96447815746831d35.svg) defined by\n\n![](images/a3/a3c2f6f5a3103e4fb023f9f433ce5fa33a215d23f5a33c8bf82b21b852e1236c.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/d5/d58f2d66646ec6255b8de257d9940476f78ea04fb65a8e0db6d19663399f0efa.svg)\n\nOur task is to find the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) under the action of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) \n\nFirst, note the following:\n\n- It's easy to check that ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) has no critical points in the *interior* of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) (we'll discuss why this matters shortly).\n\n- Since ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg)'s interior contains no critical points, we only need to find the image of its boundary ![](images/7d/7d7b652d219e930e1989bd99378c234b82ecbb8aa5eaec0574539267f3f70946.svg)\n\n- To find the image of ![](images/85/85bc8c8e3680298e08b6aecd3c4a66dcfb72f96ef1433d1bccb464af8311aee0.svg) we parametrize each *directed* edge and map them individually onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nLet's now map each directed edge of ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/37/3796e37203fb4ff84036acfaea5f94257c722a75b9b4896885548a54bd1e15ab.svg) where we have ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) and ![](images/23/233735103d4322c64d03fcb3c3c36b2968737ee86c5932190541afa9b0bf9c58.svg) Substituting ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/26/26d77a4baefb6a2d4a17264f5f8e7b9922e99cdedd62e6bfe2b08aba99ef0d17.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/35/3567fd4fae25e9a36d2a48b68dec31311753e8cec3d13b0657db727124eaae3d.svg)\nWriting these parametric equations in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/4f/4fd68f3aee9087b1c0f8b86a57fae2035fa8527d5f0b74038d7c3339e8724457.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a line segment. Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/46/468ae20a0d581d780de7cb0af719f5a34e3141c44700b0e27353df12cb001bfe.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) is a line segment traversed from the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) to ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n![](images/13/132642571953ddbfc95c3f0c399cd3c612a2ba2a84a224939b6f40e7d22a074f.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/24/24d3419f793c33397116d086271facafc3e7dc955e696abc2edbd24b307d2d6e.svg) Here, we have ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) and ![](images/7c/7cebf80c26d37190278268fc56dc3b471c289f9f9b5112adac1210a972789142.svg) Substituting ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/41/41118e0a120a0a6df82567ce1925c2b4d21b8f6c50a1c8c927382361d4ab089a.svg)\nThis horizontal line segment is traversed from the point ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/13/131644a2410e5127a69388f1bd1fced1e28698e7634b137fcd47f8b3f26121a3.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/c4/c4229f343b9b55013188ec3d2755c4f2b0517098764a1217d19ae440c0a93987.svg) Here, we have ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) and ![](images/80/8065c8e9bcaad0d0173e6a81ba9da342ceae55c13883b6ae9c9141c595b8bb7f.svg) (we write the inequality for ![](images/46/4620fefc72ebf96810ce9f348c9a728f392246ff87133b6d0e5549607fde421d.svg) in this way to show that ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is traversed *from* the point where ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) *to* the point where ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg)).\n\nSubstituting ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/7e/7ea2885f9400b3908c7dfc98f22b094126c44142421b444b156e44f42e210762.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab